The log-log specification (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) uses $\epsilon{=}10^{-6}$ to handle zero ASR values. We assess sensitivity by comparing coefficients across $\epsilon \in \{10^{-3}, 10^{-6}, 10^{-9}\}$. Zero ASR rates are low on MMLU ($<$1\% of panels across conditions) but can reach 38--40\% on ARC for authority bias and sycophancy at $K{=}7$. Coefficient magnitudes show moderate sensitivity to $\epsilon$ at $K{=}3$ (maximum $\Delta\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.10$ between $\epsilon{=}10^{-6}$ and $\epsilon{=}10^{-9}$, with three conditions exhibiting $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sign changes), reflecting the influence of zero-ASR panels on the log transformation. At $K{=}5$, all $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient directions are preserved across $\epsilon$ values (maximum $\Delta\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.073$ between $\epsilon{=}10^{-6}$ and $\epsilon{=}10^{-9}$, all $p < 0.005$), confirming that the predictor relevance pattern finding is robust to this modeling choice. The fractional logit specification (\S\ref{sec:experiments}), which avoids the log+$\epsilon$ transformation entirely, provides 98.6\% directional concordance with the main OLS results (71/72 direction matches) (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}).

\subsection{Temperature Sensitivity Pilot}

\label{sec:cross_k}

The $K{=}3$ pattern is method-sensitive (Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison}), contrasting with $K{\geq}5$ where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s association is consistent (bootstrap 8/12; fractional logit 11/12). We repeat the bootstrap at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ with max-$p$ correction.

Meta-analytic pooling \citep{dersimonian1986meta} quantifies trajectories independent of significance thresholds. $\eta_{\max}$'s pooled $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ is $0.243$ [$0.163, 0.323$] at $K{=}3$ but drops to $0.091$ [$-0.031, 0.212$] at $K{=}5$ and $0.086$ [$-0.039, 0.211$] at $K{=}7$, becoming non-significant at $K{\geq}5$ (CI crosses zero). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s pooled coefficient remains positive ($0.286$--$0.353$, CI excluding zero; Appendix~\ref{app:meta_analytic_details}). Figure~\ref{fig:regime_transition} shows significance counts; per-condition effect sizes with 95\% CIs are in Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} (Appendix~\ref{app:effect_size_forest}). The $\eta_{\max}$ decline holds under both $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\kappa$-diversity.


Random faults yield $R^2{=}0.730$; adaptive attack reduces predictability ($R^2{=}0.285$ at $K{=}5$) but $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains significance (7/12; Appendix~\ref{app:rf_contrast_detail}). The positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient reverses the BFT/Condorcet intuition: under random faults, independence helps (more independent judges $=$ lower joint failure); under adaptive adversaries, diverse panels expose a wider attack surface because the adversary can exploit heterogeneous vulnerabilities that do not overlap across judges.

\subsection{Attack-Type Heterogeneity}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM bootstrap confirms positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ association in 10/12 conditions with no magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks (Appendix~\ref{app:bft_reversal}). The two exceptions are position bias: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient is negative in both benchmarks ($\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.334, -0.372$), indicating that diverse panels are \emph{less} susceptible. Because position bias is a shared vulnerability where all judges exhibit the same directional preference, independent judges provide genuine redundancy---consistent with the Condorcet intuition and the targeted-vs-shared distinction in the attack taxonomy.

\subsection{Aggregation Rules and the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Aggregation rules influence predictability but not $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction (Table~\ref{tab:aggregation}, Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_details}). Threshold voting yields the highest $R^2$ ($0.745$), while Bayesian calibration nearly neutralizes panel composition effects ($R^2{=}0.136$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 5+/1$-$). At $K{=}5$ all methods converge (7/12; Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_k5}); at $K{=}7$ Bayesian neutralization returns (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_k7}). Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} with clustered SE confirms the association: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance is 10--11/12 across all $K$, with 98.6\% directional concordance (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}).

